concepts.body:
Scorers form a ladder from little to full control. Built-in judges need minimal setup, and Guidelines judges check natural-language rules with a pass or fail result. Custom LLM judges give full control over criteria and output, which can be numbers, categories or booleans, and suit subjective domain criteria such as a 1 to 5 regulatory-tone rubric written in prose. Judge alignment can train a custom judge to match human evaluation standards. Code-based scorers are Python functions for deterministic logic such as exact matching, format validation and performance metrics, and the same scorer can run in development evaluation and, where supported, in monitoring. The recommended definition is the @scorer decorator, with a signature using arguments such as inputs, outputs, expectations or trace:

@scorer
def has_keys(outputs):
    return {"supplier", "due_date"} <= set(json.loads(outputs))

The Scorer class is for stateful scorers or complex initialization. Third-party scorers bring metrics from open-source evaluation libraries. Parsing JSON gives the same result every time, while an LLM judge reads probabilistically, so a format check belongs in code. Production monitoring of scorers is Beta and accepts built-in judges and decorated functions, not Scorer subclasses.
